            "Refraction also affects the sunset. At the horizon, the atmosphere bends the light by about 34 arcminutes, slightly more than the Sun's diameter (32 arcminutes), so when you see the lower edge of the Sun touch the horizon, the real Sun has already set. The setting Sun is squashed to an oval, because the lower edge is bent more than the upper edge. And sometimes, in clear air and a sharp horizon, there is a last flash of green: the **green flash**.",
